{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.canada.ca/Band-tailed Pigeon (Patagioenas fasciata) COSEWIC assessment and status report 2021.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Criterion D (Very Small or Restricted Population): Not applicable. Population estimate of 43,000-300,000 mature individuals is higher than thresholds, IAO exceeds 20 km2, and number of locations likely far exceeds ten.\n"},"line_number":253,"absolute_offset":17492,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":105,"end":123}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.canada.ca/Band-tailed Pigeon (Patagioenas fasciata) COSEWIC assessment and status report 2021.txt"},"lines":{"text":"The index of area of occupancy (IAO) for Band-tailed Pigeon is unknown. Given the possible range of population estimates from 43,000-300,000 mature individuals, or 21,500-150,000 pairs (see Abundance), the IAO for Band-tailed Pigeon is likely to far exceed the threshold value of 2000 km2 for Threatened status designation based on small range. The IAO of Band-tailed Pigeon appears to be decreasing in Canada, with the disappearance of nesting birds from most sites in interior British Columbia over the past two decades (Davidson 2015).\n"},"line_number":383,"absolute_offset":31294,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":141,"end":159}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.canada.ca/Band-tailed Pigeon (Patagioenas fasciata) COSEWIC assessment and status report 2021.txt"},"lines":{"text":"The global Band-tailed Pigeon population was recently estimated to be 6.1 million mature individuals, with a North American (Canada and United States) population of about 1,500,000 mature individuals (95% CI: 1,000,000-2,000,000), and a Canadian population of about 300,000 mature individuals (95% CI: 130,000-580,000; Partners in Flight 2019). These estimates are markedly higher than the 2013 estimates from Partners in Flight (2017) (global estimate: 2.0 million mature individuals; North American estimate: 940,000 mature individuals; Canadian estimate: 160,000 mature individuals). Abundance was estimated as a product of the BBS density data and the area of the range in 2013 (Partners in Flight 2017), and as a product of the BBS density data and occupied area as indicated by eBird records in 2019 (Partners in Flight 2019). The most recent PIFSC estimate for Canada is higher than that provided by R.J. Cannings (pers. comm. in COSEWIC 2008; 43,000-170,000 mature individuals) and the range in COSEWIC (2008; 20,000-170,000 mature individuals), despite indications of a continuing decline in British Columbia (see Fluctuations and trends below).\n"},"line_number":523,"absolute_offset":60753,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":82,"end":100},{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":181,"end":199},{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":274,"end":292},{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":466,"end":484},{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":519,"end":537},{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":566,"end":584},{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":966,"end":984},{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":1033,"end":1051}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.canada.ca/Band-tailed Pigeon (Patagioenas fasciata) COSEWIC assessment and status report 2021.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Seamans (2018) noted that caution should be used in interpreting abundance estimates of Band-tailed Pigeon from BBS results, as sample sizes (number of routes) and pigeon counts per route are often low, variances may be high, and coverage of appropriate habitat by BBS routes may be poor. The 2019 abundance estimates are not used directly here, as expert opinion is more in line with the lower 2013 Canadian abundance estimate (e.g., Breault pers. comm. 2019), and it is unlikely that the declining Canadian population could represent 20% of the North American population. Instead, a broad range of 43,000-300,000 mature individuals is used here, precluding a comparison with previous abundance estimates.\n"},"line_number":525,"absolute_offset":61909,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":615,"end":633}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.canada.ca/Band-tailed Pigeon (Patagioenas fasciata) COSEWIC assessment and status report 2021.txt"},"lines":{"text":"From 1976-1990, harvest limits in British Columbia were 10 birds/day with a 20-bird possession limit, which was reduced to 5/day with a 10-bird possession limit in 1991. The Band-tailed Pigeon hunting season was closed completely in Canada from 1994 to 2001, inclusive (Gendron pers. comm. 2018; Figure 8). The current hunting season in British Columbia extends from 15-30 September, with a daily limit of five pigeons and a possession limit of 15 birds (Pacific Flyway Council 2010). Harvest is now very low In British Columbia, with a total estimated harvest of 568 individuals over the past 5 years (average of about 114 birds/year; Gendron pers. comm. 2018; Figure 8). About 66-87% of these were likely mature individuals, assuming the same age structure of harvested birds as was reported in the United States by Sanders and Braun (2014).\n"},"line_number":635,"absolute_offset":77080,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":707,"end":725}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.canada.ca/Band-tailed Pigeon (Patagioenas fasciata) COSEWIC assessment and status report 2021.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Based on the timing and duration of US hunting seasons, the Canadian population of Pacific Coast Band-tailed Pigeon may also be exposed to some hunting pressure in, and en route to, overwintering areas in California, although harvest in the United States is likely to be very low. In 2018, the 9-day hunting season extended from 15-23 September in Washington, Oregon, and northern California, and from 15-23 December in southern California, with daily bag limits varying from 2-10 birds and a possession limit of three times the daily bag limit (Seamans 2018). The average annual harvest level in California, Oregon, and Washington was about 8200 total individuals over the recent period 2012-2016, or about 6200 mature individuals (Seamans 2018). Impacts of crippling loss and illegal hunting on Band-tailed Pigeon in the United States are unknown but may be locally important (Braun pers. comm. 2019), although additional impact on birds from Canada is likely negligible.\n"},"line_number":641,"absolute_offset":78072,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":713,"end":731}]}}
