{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"datazone.birdlife.org/Many threatened birds have small ranges, often on small islands.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Many threatened birds have small or very small ranges (see figure 1). Thirty-five threatened species have an occupied range of less than 10 km2 (BirdLife International 2023). For example, Floreana Mockingbird Mimus trifasciatus is restricted to two tiny islets totalling just 0.9 km2 in the Galapagos Islands, Ecuador. Altogether, 328 threatened bird species (23%) qualify as threatened because they have small ranges and are declining, fragmented, restricted to a few locations, or undergoing extreme fluctuations. Many of these species are restricted to just a few locations—for example Taita Thrush Turdus helleri is confined to four tiny forest fragments in the Taita Hills, Kenya. The threatened birds that have very large ranges (258 species have range sizes over one million km2) are considered threatened either because they have undergone steep population declines, or because they occur at very low densities and have small declining populations. For example, the Lappet-faced Vulture Torgos tracheliotus has a range of 34 million km2 across much of Africa and the Middle East, but it is estimated that only 6,500 mature individuals remain, and this number is declining.\n"},"line_number":9,"absolute_offset":573,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":1126,"end":1144}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"datazone.birdlife.org/Many threatened birds have small ranges, often on small islands.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Island species are also more vulnerable than continental species to marine natural disasters, such as hurricanes, due to the usually low-lying land and lack of wind or rain buffers. The Tuamotu Kingfisher Todiramphus gambieri, endemic to Niau Island, French Polynesia, is a perfect example of oceanic island species' struggles. It is Critically Endangered with only 90 remaining mature individuals, a number that is currently decreasing (BirdLife International 2023). Predominant causes for this small population are predation by feral cats Felis catus and loss of suitable nesting sites due to hurricanes (Gouni et al. 2006).\n"},"line_number":17,"absolute_offset":3251,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"mature individuals"},"start":379,"end":397}]}}
